{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents adding, changing, or removing end-user-observable behavior that no agreed requirement already specifies in a Habeas project to draft requirements and commit them, once agreed under {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.agreement` and {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.commit`, before writing implementation or test code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The three requirements (`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill`, `.agreement` and `.commit`) prescribe what the `habeas-requirements` skill tells agents to do. They don't prescribe libraries, algorithms, signatures, types or named interfaces. The only structural clause is that the skill is named `habeas-requirements`. `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` has that name in its frontmatter, and the supplied root is the skill itself.\n\nI checked the skill against each clause. The skill file is the whole implementation, so I found no further implementation roots to follow.\n- **Draft and commit before code** (`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill`): `SKILL.md` Workflow step 1 sends an agent straight to implementation when no observable behavior changes or an existing requirement already covers it. Step 2 drafts requirements into the chapter, step 5 commits them, and step 6 implements only afterwards.\n- **Agreement gate** (`.agreement`): the step 4 Gate says there is no implementation or test code and no commit until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group.\n- **One commit** (`.commit`): step 5 reverts rejected or deferred drafts. It then commits the agreed text of all groups together before any code, and leaves out the unrelated edits found in step 1.\n\nI considered an alternative skill whose directives read differently but produce the same observable agent behavior. Its text would differ, but it would still meet the clauses, so the wording is discretionary. I therefore found no structural obligation that needs `{impl}` evidence.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one behavioral obligation. The `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents who add, change or remove end-user-observable behavior that no agreed requirement already specifies to draft requirements and commit them, once agreed, before writing implementation or test code. The agreement gate and the single commit are refined by `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.agreement` and `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.commit`, so they need no test here.\n\nTwo tests cover it.\n\n1. `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_commits_agreed_requirements_before_code]`:\n   - It asserts the skill says every end-user-observable result of the code must follow from an agreed requirement.\n   - It locates the workflow steps for \"Write drafts\", the review subagent, the **Gate**, \"then commit it, all groups together, before any code\" and \"Implement and test\". `step_of` panics if a phrase is missing.\n   - It asserts `draft < review < gate < commit < implement`. This enforces the \"draft, then commit once agreed, before implementation or test code\" ordering. A skill that put implementation or commit earlier, or dropped drafting, would fail.\n   - I read the actual `SKILL.md` steps 2 (Draft), 4 (Gate), 5 (Commit, \"before any code\") and 6 (Implement and test). They match these phrases.\n\n2. `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_reviews_every_change_to_agreed_text]`:\n   - It covers the scoping condition, \"that no agreed requirement already specifies\". It asserts the skill's exemptions for a change that adds, alters or removes no observable behavior, and for behavior an existing requirement already specifies (the skill tells the user which requirement covers it).\n   - It asserts the description says to use the skill \"before changing code, tests, or requirement text\".\n   - It asserts that changes to agreed text, including removing a requirement, go through steps 3 to 5 before the commit. This covers the \"changing or removing\" cases.\n\nI found no behavior that violates the requirement and still passes these tests. No acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "1869e93b2fb32934934787f1b2d9b5582e09c00b67a606e38133c7b50fca1e61",
